    "created_at": "2018-04-09T13:26:13Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-04-09T13:26:13Z",
    "body": "Declared on what?\r\n\r\nIf you have an SVG image, with CSS inside the SVG that styles its root, that gives the SVG intrinsic dimensions (in the sense the CSS specs use the term) from the point of view of the document the SVG image is embedded in (via `<img>` tag).\r\n\r\nIf you have some CSS in a document that just styles an element in that same document, that does _not_ give it intrinsic dimensions in the CSS sense.\r\n\r\nPut another way, \"intrinsic dimensions\" are that thing that `img.naturalWidth/naturalHeight` return (clearly stated so at <https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#dom-img-naturalheight>).\r\n\r\nOr do you mean that having CSS width/height defined should affect what sort of box an image gets?  That is a good question, and the discussion about that led me to filing this bug.  There is no place in the web platform so far where the width/height CSS properties affect the types of CSS boxes.  Adding such a thing would require significant deoptimization of changes to those properties, which is not really acceptable.\r\n\r\nPast that, I have no strong opinions, for the \"no alt text\" case.  Right now the spec aims to have no alt text and not representing an image and no intrinsic dimensions (that part is redundant) be treated as an empty inline element, but I think Firefox is the only browser that does that, and it's actually causing us compat problems.  So maybe the right answer is that if the image represents nothing and has no alt text it should be treated like a replaced element of intrisic size 0x0?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
